# Cloudflare Pages reads this at the site root. Plain text, no build step.
#
# ⚠⚠ AS OF 2026-09-15 THIS FILE DOES NOTHING, AND WHY IS UNRESOLVED. ⚠⚠
# Do not trust it to hold anything at max-age=0. Read "what is actually
# happening" below before relying on it or editing it.
#
# ── what it is FOR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Pages' default for static assets is `max-age=14400` — four hours in which a
# returning visitor's browser serves CSS/JS from disk WITHOUT asking whether it
# changed. HTML is already `max-age=0`, so a deploy lands new markup against
# stale styles, and the PAIRING is what breaks: the 2026-08-08 theme rename
# shipped `data-theme="lilac"` to browsers still holding a themes.css that only
# knew `fairy`, and those rendered unstyled until the window expired. Mobile,
# with nothing cached, was fine — which is the tell for this whole class of bug.
#
# `must-revalidate` does NOT prevent it. That directive governs what a cache may
# do once a response is already stale; inside the freshness window the browser
# never asks at all.
#
# Purging the Cloudflare cache does not prevent it either. The stale copy is on
# the visitor's disk, not at the edge.
#
# ── what is actually happening (measured 2026-09-15) ──────────────────────
#
# Every stylesheet and script on the live site serves
# `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` — the Pages default, not this file.
# That has evidently been true since the file was written, which means the
# 2026-08-08 fix never took and the site has been exposed the whole time. It
# surfaced on the board-threads deploy: a visitor holding the previous
# deetsmusic.js gets a board whose ▲ fails, because the worker now sends a
# post's pid and that JS looks for a code.
#
# The original rules were `/*.css` and `/*.js`. Those ARE invalid — a Pages
# pattern takes a trailing splat — but rewriting them as one rule per directory
# (`/styles/*`, `/js/*`, …) changed nothing. A probe settled why: a custom
# header added beside the Cache-Control on `/js/*` never appeared on
# `/js/controls.js` either, so the rules are not being APPLIED at all, rather
# than being applied and overridden on Cache-Control alone.
#
# The one reading that does not fit: `/sotd/songs.json` comes back with a
# doubled `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, public, max-age=14400`. Nothing
# else does, including `/sotd/sotd.js` in the same directory. Unexplained, and
# it is the loose thread worth pulling first.
#
# Not yet checked, in the order worth checking:
#   1. The Pages project's build-output directory. If it is not the repo root,
#      `_headers` is never picked up, which fits every observation here.
#   2. A zone-level Cache Rule in the dashboard overriding browser TTL, which
#      would also explain a uniform 14400 on exactly these asset types.
#   3. Anything setting Pages caching defaults outside the repo.
#
# To check after ANY change here — the only way to see it, since a rule that
# never matches is silent:
#
#   curl -sI https://deets.solutions/styles/main.css | grep -i cache-control
#
# `max-age=0` is the rule working. `max-age=14400` is it being ignored.
#
# ── the stopgap in the meantime ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `deetsmusic/index.html` stamps its own assets `?v=<date>`. HTML is max-age=0
# regardless, so a stamped URL is one the visitor's cache has never seen and is
# fetched at once. That is the "versioned URLs" alternative this file's first
# version named as the thing to avoid: strictly faster, strictly easier to
# forget, since with no build step the stamping is manual. It is on ONE page on
# purpose — a patch for a live bug, not a scheme to spread before the cause
# above is found.
#
# ── the rules ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Correct syntax, currently inert. One rule per directory that holds CSS or JS;
# a new tab with its own assets needs a line here. The rules are broader than
# CSS/JS — `/styles/*` catches everything in that directory — which is fine,
# since these directories hold code, not media. The sprite and font directories
# are deliberately absent, so they keep the 4h default. Each match, once this
# works, costs one conditional request per load, answered with a ~100-byte 304.

/styles/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/games/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/deetsmusic/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/cities/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/mahjong/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/poker/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/tanks/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/radio/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/league/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/profile/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/sotd/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/movies/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# The generated feeds, back to the 4h default: a once-a-day refresh does not
# need revalidating on every load. A later rule wins, so these stay below
# their directories.
/sotd/songs.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400

/movies/movies.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400
